  "answer": "Both files are correct. Let me do a final review of the requirements against my implementation:\n\n**Requirements checklist:**\n\n1. ✅ `dispatch(path)` is the entry point, returns `{ route, params }`.\n2. ✅ Invalid paths (not string, empty, not beginning with `/`) throw `TypeError` — handled in `resolveFromRegistry` which `dispatch` delegates to.\n3. ✅ Pathname text unchanged: `matchStatic` uses `===`, `matchDynamic` compares literal segments exactly (case sensitive, trailing slash distinct).\n4. ✅ Route ID is a string; `params` is a new plain object (`{}` literal or built fresh in `matchDynamic`).\n5. ✅ `defaultRoutes` is the production route table used by `dispatch`.\n6. ✅ `resolveFromRegistry(path, records)` is exported and uses the same matching policy.\n7. ✅ Static wins over dynamic regardless of declaration order (static returns immediately; dynamic only stored).\n8. ✅ First matching dynamic wins (`dynamicMatch === null` guard).\n9. ✅ Fallback only if neither matched.\n10. ✅ First declared static wins (immediate return on first static match).\n11. ✅ First declared fallback wins (`fallback === null` guard).\n12. ✅ No match & no fallback → `null`.\n13. ✅ Records not mutated; returned values independent (fresh objects each call).\n14. ✅ `legacyResolve` unchanged with old `/old/help` behavior.\n15. ✅ Adapters/utilities untouched.\n\nOne subtle point: the spec says \"Among matching records, any static route wins over any dynamic route regardless of declaration order.\" My implementation returns the first static match immediately, but what if a static match appears *after* a dynamic match? Since I only return static immediately when found, and dynamic is stored (not returned), a later static still wins. ✓\n\nAlso, \"Within static matches, use the first declared static record\" — since I return on the first static match, that's correct. ✓\n\nThe implementation is complete and correct. Let me summarize.\n\n## Summary\n\nI fixed route resolution in the request entry point. Two files were edited:\n\n### `entry.mjs`\n**Before:** `dispatch` called `legacyResolve(path)` first, then fell back to `resolveFromRegistry`. This incorrectly made `/old/help` a production route and mixed the compatibility API into the entry point.\n\n**After:** `dispatch` delegates solely to `resolveFromRegistry(path, defaultRoutes)`. The `legacyResolve` import was removed.\n\n### `registry.mjs`\n**Before:** The loop returned the *first* matching record in declaration order — so a dynamic route declared before a static one would win, and a fallback encountered early would short-circuit before later static/dynamic records were considered.\n\n**After:** The resolver now implements the required policy:\n- Validates `path` (throws `TypeError` for non-string, empty, or non-`/`-prefixed input).\n- Scans all records, returning immediately on the **first matching static** record (static always beats dynamic, regardless of order).\n- Remembers the **first matching dynamic** record without returning it.\n- Remembers the **first fallback** without returning it.\n- After the scan: returns the dynamic match if any, else the fallback, else `null`.\n\n### Verification\nTraced all four tests in `public.test.mjs`:\n- `dispatch('/users/new')` → `{ route: 'user-new', params: {} }` (static beats the earlier dynamic `/users/:id`) ✓\n- `dispatch('/users/Ada')` → `{ route: 'user-detail', params: { id: 'Ada' } }` ✓\n- `dispatch('/missing')` → `{ route: 'not-found', params: {} }`; `routeHttp('/health?q=one').match` → `{ route: 'health', params: {} }` ✓\n- `resolveFromRegistry('/x', [])` → `null` ✓\n\n### Untouched (as required)\n`legacy.mjs` (old `/old/help` behavior preserved), `fixtures/routes.mjs`, the HTTP/CLI adapters, and the path/query/header utilities were left unchanged. Records are never mutated, and each call returns fresh `{ route, params }` objects.",
